{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"IMPLEMENTED — Gerrit change 1344 (https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1344), NOT submitted, ticket left in_progress.\n\nWHERE THE DECISION LIVES, AND WHY. `_anthropic_web_search_capabilities` is DELETED from anthropic_model.py; the decision is now `capabilities.web_search_capabilities(*, web: bool)`. Two reasons for that home: (1) it is no longer an Anthropic construction detail but a capability decision, the same shape as `cache_settings_for(caps, *, execution_mode)` which already lives there; (2) anthropic_model.py's own docstring scopes it to the DIRECT-Anthropic construction path, which this no longer is. The function takes NO model or provider argument at all — web access is required on every provider, so nothing about the provider is an input, and a function handed no model string cannot gate on the shape of one. The runner threads `web_requested = bool(req.web) and not self._model_override` into BOTH the capability call and provenance, so the attached capability and the record that attests it cannot disagree. Renaming was checked with grep for the NAME AS A STRING (not just a symbol search): the only reference was agent_call.py's monkeypatch-hazard docstring, updated; no test monkeypatched the old name.\n\nMECHANISM: one `WebSearch(native=WebSearchTool(max_uses=5), local=duckduckgo_search_tool(max_results=5))`. pydantic-ai keeps the native tool where `profile.supported_native_tools` contains WebSearchTool and drops it for the local tool where it does not — so native is preferred (cheaper, provider-grounded, no third-party text in our process) and local is the floor. NativeOrLocalTool was NOT needed; WebSearch already IS one.\n\nA TRAP WORTH RECORDING. Setting max_uses / blocked_domains / allowed_domains at the CAPABILITY level flips pydantic-ai's `_requires_native()` True, and `NativeOrLocalTool.get_toolset()` then returns None — i.e. the local fallback is SUPPRESSED and the run hard-fails on any provider without the native tool. Hardening the capability the obvious way would have re-created this exact bug. max_uses therefore rides the NATIVE TOOL INSTANCE, where `_requires_native()` does not see it; a test asserts `get_toolset() is not None` so a future edit cannot silently reintroduce that.\n\nSECURITY POSTURE (argued in a block comment above the function, not just here). Fetched content is UNTRUSTED INPUT to a BLOCKING gate. Bounds: VOLUME on both routes (native max_uses=5 per run; local max_results=5 per call; plus the agentic loop's own request/step budget). SHAPE on the local route — pydantic-ai's DuckDuckGo tool returns title+href+snippet ONLY, never page bodies, so this is a SEARCH capability, not the unbounded homegrown HTTP fetch tool the original design was protecting against, and no rebar-side fetcher was written. AUTHORITY downstream — output is validated against the criteria finding contract, and T1's rubric now states results are untrusted data. DOMAIN CONTROLS DELIBERATELY UNUSED, and this is a tradeoff not an omission: an allowlist is self-defeating for a criterion whose job is discovering UNKNOWN prior art, and a blocklist cannot hold uniformly (capability-level kills the fallback per the trap above; native-instance-level binds only one route, and a control that holds on one route and not the other is a misleading control).\n\nOBSERVABILITY: `provider_provenance.capabilities.web_access` is now recorded as \"native\" | \"local\" | \"off\". Three values not a boolean, because the two ON routes are not equivalent (only \"local\" means third-party text entered rebar's process). `off` wins whenever the run did not attach the capability, so the record can never attest grounding a reviewer did not have. New profile-derived field `ModelCapabilities.native_web_search`, read by MEMBERSHIP in `supported_native_tools` — the same registry pydantic-ai filters against — and intersected with `all()` across a fallback chain.\n\nPACKAGING, PROVEN BY EXECUTION NOT BY READING. `pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic,retries,duckduckgo]` — folded into the `agents` extra ITSELF rather than a new opt-in extra, so no deployment can install the agents runtime and forget it; that also means Dockerfile.reviewbot's `uv sync --locked --no-dev --extra agents --extra reviewbot --extra bedrock` needed NO new flag (a comment there records why, so a future narrowing of `agents` does not silently drop it). uv.lock regenerated and committed (+ddgs 9.14.4, lxml, primp, brotli/brotlicffi, fake-useragent, h2/hpack/hyperframe, socksio). EXECUTED in a venv synced from the new lock: `import pydantic_ai.common_tools.duckduckgo` OK; ddgs 9.14.4; the capability CONSTRUCTS with max_uses=5 and local tool 'duckduckgo_search'; get_native_tools()==['WebSearchTool'] and get_toolset() is not None; web=False -> None. Route read per provider: anthropic/openai/google -> native; bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8 -> local (`supported_native_tools` is EMPTY, agreeing with the live Bedrock measurement on this ticket). pip-audit: no known vulnerabilities.\n\nT1 RUBRIC: the hedge is gone (\"MAY be offered\", \"provider-dependent\", \"when it is not available\", \"fall back\" all removed) and replaced with an assertion that the tool IS available. The anti-fabrication clause is KEPT and strengthened (\"cite only sources you actually retrieved\"), plus a new clause naming results untrusted data, not instructions.\n\nTESTS — tests/unit/test_web_tool_gating.py rewritten (20 tests), each family verified RED by mutation:\n* reinstating the anthropic prefix gate -> 5 RED, including `test_runner_attaches_web_capability_on_every_provider[bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8]` (\"web-flagged request on bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8 got NO web capability\"), the openai and google params, `test_the_decision_takes_no_model_or_provider_input`, and the call-site AST guard (\"model/provider passed positionally\").\n* deleting the provenance field -> RED, `KeyError: 'web_access'`.\n* making web=False attach anyway -> 4 RED, `assert 'capabilities' not in unflagged` on all four providers (the no-request path is byte-identical).\n* reverting the T1 rubric from git HEAD -> RED, \"T1 still frames web access as optional: 'may be offered'\"; dropping only the untrusted clause -> RED on that assertion alone.\nThe attested guard `test_capabilities_module_still_has_no_provider_name_prefix_matching` is untouched and passes; my call-site guard deliberately does not duplicate or weaken it. One pre-existing test updated: test_verdict_provenance.py's exact-dict assertion gained `web_access: off` (with a contradictory native_web_search=True to prove the record does not leak capability as usage).\n\nGATES, all by exit code on a CLEAN tree (`git status --porcelain` empty), re-run after rebasing onto origin/main 5bc3a432d7: make lint 0 · make typecheck 0 · check_comment_hygiene 0 · check_config_reads 0 · check_docs_index 0 · check_raw_git_writes 0 · check_readme_quickstart 0 · check_server_manifest 0 · check_verify_gate_parity 0 · check_wall_clock_asserts 0 · uv lock --check 0 · make config-check 0 · pre-commit run --all-files 0 · pytest -m 'not integration and not external' -n 6 --dist worksteal 0 (9863 passed, 34 skipped).\n\nSTILL OPEN, for whoever closes this: acceptance criterion 2 says web access is verified 'by a run on each configured provider, not by inspection of the gate condition'. What I have is the attach path exercised per provider through the runner with the Agent construction captured, plus the route derived from each provider's real profile — no live model call was made on any provider (no spend). A live Bedrock plan-review run showing `web_access: \"local\"` on a signed verdict would close that criterion outright."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785812073170820001,"uuid":"92f5c6f8-1801-44fd-975b-61515b632cc3"}